Ladywood is one of the most deprived constituencies in Birmingham and the second most deprived in the city overall. Food insecurity is a defining challenge: 11.8% of Birmingham residents struggle to access food, 12.4% worry about having enough. High food insecurity pushes residents towards cheap, highly processed options, compounding long-term health problems in a neighbourhood that already scores 16 out of 20 on the Biological Inequities Index.
Despite this crisis of access, Birmingham holds more allotment plots than any other local authority in the UK 113 sites and nearly 7,000 plots. Many allotment holders grow more than they can consume, and surplus produce regularly goes to waste.
The EWB brief identifies food, digital inclusion, and transport as interconnected challenge areas in Ladywood. Crop connect addresses all three simultaneously within a single community-owned platform.
The core problem is therefore a disconnection between the surplus of locally grown food and the people experiencing food insecurity, exacerbated by language barriers, poor digital inclusion, and a lack of a centralised system linking allotment holders, food banks, and residents.
